    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To stifle competition, companies may engage in \"self-preferencing,\" where they favor their own products in search results or app stores over competitors'. They can also use their massive data troves to unfairly develop competing products, effectively locking out smaller players. Conversely, critics of regulation suggest that stringent rules impose high compliance costs, which disproportionately affect smaller, agile startups. Overly restrictive government oversight could also discourage the very risks and rapid experimentation that fuel disruptive technological innovation.",
      "source": "target",
